6-23",56.26],["2026-06-24",56.13],["2026-06-25",55.62],["2026-06-29",55.53],["2026-06-30",53.54],["2026-07-01",52.9],["2026-07-02",53.95],["2026-07-03",53.13],["2026-07-06",53.25],["2026-07-07",53.54],["2026-07-08",51.8],["2026-07-09",51.7],["2026-07-10",51.58]],"qtr":"FY26","ratios":[{"label":"ROE %","value":"0.6%"},{"label":"ROCE %","value":"2.5%"},{"label":"D/E","value":"1.4x"},{"label":"Int cover","value":"0.4x"},{"label":"Receivable d","value":"30.7"},{"label":"Inventory d","value":"126.6"},{"label":"Payable d","value":"33.9"},{"label":"OPM %","value":"3.3%"},{"label":"Promoter %","value":"72.5%"}],"s":"KHAICHEM","sec":"Fertilizer Manufacturing (SSP & Sulphuric Acid)","sec2":"Chemicals","sections":{"bull_bear":[{"body":"Prioritizing working capital needs and debt servicing; no dividends or buybacks indicated; reliance on related-party loans (INR 220M as of 9MFY26) to manage liquidity.","src":"concall","title":"Capital allocation"},{"body":"1. Sulphur price volatility (USD 500–600/ton vs. FY25 avg USD 150–160); 2. Delayed or reduced government subsidy disbursement; 3. Capped DAP prices limiting SSP price flexibility; 4. High working capital utilization (84%) and low cash balance (INR 0.8M); 5. Geopolitical disruption in West Asia affecting raw material supply.","src":"concall","title":"Risks & watch-items"},{"body":"n/a (non-concall source)","src":"concall","title":"Management quality / tone"},{"body":"Despite negative EBITDA in prior years, KCFL's profitability in 9MFY26 was driven by a one-time subsidy jump (NBS increased from INR 5,122 to INR 8,149/ton), masking structural cost issues; sulphuric acid margins are a hidden bright spot but too small to offset SSP losses.","src":"concall","title":"Non-obvious takeaways"},{"body":"- Maintains a healthy balance sheet with low long-term debt; net debt to equity improved from 1.4x in FY24 to 1.0x in FY26.\n- Capital allocation philosophy focuses on financial discipline to strengthen the balance sheet and build a foundation for sustainable long-term growth.","src":"deck","title":"Capital allocation policy (investor deck)"}],"business":[{"body":"SSP: 88% of revenue (FY25), 1.13 mtpa capacity, 44% utilization in 9MFY26; Sulphuric Acid: 11% of revenue, EBITDA/ton rose to INR 3,852 in 9MFY26 from INR 2,068 in FY25; by-product steam powers 4.32MW unit.","src":"concall","title":"Segment / subsidiary detail"},{"body":"| — | — | — | — |","src":"concall","title":"Challenges → beneficiaries & losers"},{"body":"- Holds ~10% share of India's SSP market, making it the second-largest producer nationally with over 40 years of industry presence.\n- Markets SSP under trusted umbrella brands 'Khaitan' and 'Utsav', supported by a network of 3,000+ dealers and 30,000+ retailers across 19 states.","src":"deck","title":"Positioning (investor deck)"},{"body":"- [Industry Leadership] Chairman Mr. Shailesh Khaitan serves on the FAI board and chaired the SSP Advisory Committee (2009–2023), providing policy-level influence in the fertilizer sector.\n- [Regulatory Tailwind] Kharif 2026 nutrient subsidy rates increased ~10% for N, P, and S vs. Rabi 2025, and freight subsidy for SSP continued, supporting affordability and demand.\n- [Soya Business Exit] Soya processing operations at Ratlam and Tri-Star were discontinued in 2012 due to declining profitability; significant land retained, and a discontinued operations loss of INR 42 Mn was recognized in FY26.","src":"deck","title":"Notable from the deck"}],"latest_quarter":[{"body":"Revenue: INR 8,237M (FY25); EBITDA: INR 1,216M (FY25); PAT: Not disclosed; Net Debt: INR 1,828M (FY25); Credit Rating: Ind-Ra, IND BBB-, Rating Watch with Developing Implications; Rationale: Constrained financial flexibility, thin SSP profitability amid raw material inflation, high working capital utilization (84%), and subsidy-dependent cash flows.","src":"concall","title":"Financial scorecard / credit view"},{"body":"SSP gross margin: negative under current subsidy (INR 8,149/ton) vs. cost of INR 15,500–16,500/ton (raw material + manufacturing); SA margin improved significantly due to higher realized prices and lower input costs relative to SSP.","src":"concall","title":"Margins & cost dynamics"}],"outlook":[{"body":"Outlook hinges on ability to raise SSP retail prices without losing volume, consistent raw material supply, and timely subsidy releases; no formal guidance issued beyond monitoring these factors.","src":"concall","title":"Guidance / outlook"},{"body":"Not disclosed; sales volume for SSP was 0.38 mt in 9MFY26, up from 0.34 mt in FY24, but production halted at three plants due to cost constraints.","src":"concall","title":"Order book & pipeline"},{"body":"None identified; expansion constrained by capital intensity, subsidy dependency, and raw material volatility; potential upside only if SSP pricing power is restored and sulphur costs stabilize.","src":"concall","title":"Growth verticals / optionality"}]},"sources":["FY26 concall summary","structured quarterly financials","investor presentation (24-04-2026)","credit-rating action (India Ratings and Research (Ind-Ra) 24-04-2026)"],"thesis":"Khaitan Chemicals faces severe margin pressure on its core SSP business due to soaring sulphur prices and capped retail pricing, despite improved EBITDA from sulphuric acid and subsidy hikes; liquidity remains fragile with thin cash balances and high working capital dependency, making its financial stability contingent on timely subsidy disbursement and ability to pass on costs.","track":{"expired_n":0,"open":[],"open_total":0,"quarters_n":0,"revisions":[],"verdicts":[],"verdicts_total":0},"val5":[{"de":0.45,"fy":"FY21","mcap":213.0,"opm":12.9,"pe":8.6,"roe":16.5},{"de":0.54,"fy":"FY22","mcap":1222.0,"opm":14.9,"pe":15.4,"roe":37.6},{"de":0.75,"fy":"FY23","mcap":650.0,"opm":9.0,"pe":15.4,"roe":13.7},{"de":1.4,"fy":"FY24","mcap":623.0,"opm":-5.3,"pe":null,"roe":-27.4},{"de":1.41,"fy":"FY25","mcap":449.0,"opm":3.3,"pe":320.7,"roe":0.6}],"wtt":null},"meta":{"co":"KHAITAN CHEM & FERT LTD","isin":"INE745B01028","s":"KHAICHEM","sector":"Chemicals"},"s":"KHAICHEM"}